{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request fbee7239: yes to the partition and to the combined\nrequest route.\n\n1. Partition. FH-1 (promise 1be80098) owns five paths:\n   `.githooks/commit-msg`, `.githooks/pre-commit`,\n   `scripts/pre-commit.mjs`, `scripts/git-hooks.test.mjs` and its\n   implementation note. GS-3 (promise a187a745) owns the other 17,\n   with the shared testing docs and its own note.\n2. Filing. Publish each path one time, in two batches, each note\n   last. File from a script and print each result.\n3. Request. One `gs state` request to the checker that rests on all\n   22 artifacts, the GS-3 primary first, with both reporting notes.\n   State in its text that one verdict must name both\n   implementations.\n4. Review and landing. The checker runs `gs review --prepare` and\n   gives one guarded verdict with both `--implementation` selectors.\n   The merge must bind both reports. Read back both closures after\n   the landing and state them in the landing END.\n5. A refusal at prepare, review or merge stops the route. Send the\n   refusal text in one act. Do not retry with other flags and do not\n   relabel a promise. I decide the next route then.\n\nReason. A split into one head for each lane cannot land: the merge\nplan needs every changed path under the approval, and each head\nwould hold the paths of the other lane.\n\nThe gate slot is free since my END 61245759. The first ready head\ntakes it.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}